+# Agent Note: Resource workspaces and authored context
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-12-resource-workspaces-and-authored-context.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Resource-backed conversations need stable target identities independent of process cwd. Authored assistant messages must not invent model attempts. Input validation must run before durable queue acceptance, while configuration changes need the same idle exclusion.
+
+## Decision
+
+Workspace providers resolve targets and validate durable session membership. Directory targets retain canonical-path validation. The registry rejects old domain formats. Session creation and input admission expose generic hooks; preset selection validates before recomposition and reconciles associations after durable selection. Authored context is committed after system messages and before accepted inputs, preserving message IDs, roles and producer metadata. Chat definitions may keep a node independent of process disclosure.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+Treating resources as directories would couple grouping to filesystem access. Fake assistant attempts would distort usage and request reconstruction. Always resolving the latest configuration would destroy historical provenance. A user-message envelope for every role would change model semantics. Plugin-owned loops would duplicate queue, cancellation and persistence behavior.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session records preserve complete authored input without adding model usage. Required event recognition rejects readers that do not know the new events. Existing directory navigation remains supported. Resource deletion affects availability, not retained transcript identity. The generic hooks contain no application-specific data rules.
+
+Installed context records require current validation before the released relationship checker can inspect their message positions. A private user-role view preserves coordinates for that second check; it is neither written nor returned. Original roles and admission metadata survive restoration. Invalid current roles, metadata and surface references fail before the view can be used.
